```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int X, Y;
    std::cout << "Enter two positive integers X and Y: ";
    std::cin >> X >> Y;

    while (X != 0) {
        if (X > Y) {
            std::swap(X, Y);
        } else {
            X = Y - X;
            Y = X;
        }
    }

    std::cout << "Final value of Y: " << Y << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This C++ program takes two positive integers X and Y as input, performs the given operation until X becomes 0, and then outputs the final value of Y. The `std::swap` function is used to swap X and Y when X is greater than Y, and the assignment statements are used to update X and Y otherwise.

